&lt;p&gt;Yonto Player talks to a media server, or to a folder, through one interface: &lt;code&gt;ContentSourceAdapter&lt;/code&gt;. The rule itself is covered in the opening post of the series. This one is about what sits behind the interface, and how it changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The short version is that the source contract started out as an HTTP API, moved into the app as JavaScript plugins, and is now the way every Jellyfin, Plex and Emby source works. I will say why, what a plugin looks like, and what running JavaScript inside a TV app costs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


It started as an HTTP contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first idea was a contract in JSON. A source is anything that can answer a few questions: what categories do you have, what is in this one, tell me about this title, search for this. That is written down as &lt;code&gt;contracts/content-source-http.md&lt;/code&gt; in the &lt;a href="https://github.com/yonto-app/yonto-player-plugins" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;plugins repo&lt;/a&gt;, with schemas beside it, and an HTTP adapter in the app speaks it to a hosted service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It works, and it is still there for anyone who wants to run a service of their own. But it has a cost for the most common case. To read a Jellyfin server I would need a service in the middle that talks Jellyfin on one side and my contract on the other, and someone has to run it. Every field a media server renames becomes a fix in that service and then a deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea that changed it: keep the contract's JSON shapes as the one definition, and make the transport an implementation detail. HTTPS to a service is one transport. A call into a JavaScript engine inside the app is another. The code that turns the JSON into the app's titles, details and playback options is shared and knows about neither.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once that was true, the sources that model somebody else's system had no reason to be Kotlin. A Jellyfin or Emby server has its own idea of what an item is, and that idea changes on the server's schedule, not mine. Putting that knowledge in a file instead of in the app meant two things: an update to a source can be a plugin file rather than a new build of the app, and the app keeps one way for third-party sources instead of two. The exceptions are small on purpose: the fallback shown when nothing is configured, the adapter for the viewer's own files, and the adapter that speaks our own HTTP contract, which has no third-party system to keep up with.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a plugin is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plugin is one JavaScript file. Its manifest, which says who it is and which hosts it may reach, is a comment at the top. The app reads that comment as text and never runs the file to learn it, which is why a permission lives in the manifest and not in code. The rest of the file exports functions the host calls: four are required (&lt;code&gt;getCategories&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;getMediaList&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;getMediaDetail&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;search&lt;/code&gt;) and the rest, like filters, recommendations, a health check and playback reports, are optional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the Jellyfin plugin's whole &lt;code&gt;search&lt;/code&gt; method, verbatim from the &lt;a href="https://github.com/yonto-app/yonto-player-plugins" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;public repo&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ME&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;searchTerm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Recursive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;IncludeItemTypes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ALL_ITEM_TYPES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;LIST_ITEM_FIELDS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;EnableUserData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PAGE_SIZE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toSummary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is nothing Android in it. It asks the server a question and maps the answer to the contract's summaries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The host API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plugin reaches the outside world through one global, &lt;code&gt;yonto&lt;/code&gt;, and nothing else. There is no &lt;code&gt;URL&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Buffer&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;console&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt;, and a plugin cannot read a file or open a socket. What it gets is &lt;code&gt;yonto.fetch&lt;/code&gt; for requests, &lt;code&gt;yonto.config&lt;/code&gt; for the settings the viewer filled in, &lt;code&gt;yonto.store&lt;/code&gt; for a small cache, &lt;code&gt;yonto.log&lt;/code&gt;, HTML and XML parsing, and some hashing and encoding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Errors are part of the contract too. A plugin does not return a string that looks like an error; it throws one of a few typed errors, and the app picks the screen:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;404&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;yonto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;notFound&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;what&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;yonto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;SERVER_ERROR_MESSAGE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That is also from the Jellyfin plugin, with a logging line left out. &lt;code&gt;notFound&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;unauthenticated&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;misconfigured&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;unreachable&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;unavailable&lt;/code&gt; each mean something different to the viewer, such as whether to offer a retry, so the plugin says what happened and the app decides how to show it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The settings form works the same way. A plugin declares its fields in the manifest (&lt;code&gt;text&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;secret&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;url&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;choice&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;bool&lt;/code&gt;) and the host draws the form, so the app does not gain a screen for every new source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Testing without a TV
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part I care about most is that a plugin can be checked on a laptop. The command-line tool in the same repo, &lt;code&gt;yonto-plugin&lt;/code&gt;, runs a plugin against the same contract, the same &lt;code&gt;yonto&lt;/code&gt; global and the same limits as the app:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;yonto-plugin init my-source
yonto-plugin lint
yonto-plugin run &lt;span class="nb"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; search &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'"sourdough"'&lt;/span&gt;
yonto-plugin doctor &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--record&lt;/span&gt;
yonto-plugin doctor &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--replay&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;init&lt;/code&gt; scaffolds a plugin that already passes. &lt;code&gt;lint&lt;/code&gt; checks the manifest against its schema, builds the file and works out the contract version the code needs, without making a request. The contract version is a number in the manifest saying which part of the plugin interface the plugin uses, so an app too old to run it can turn it away. &lt;code&gt;run&lt;/code&gt; calls one function and prints its JSON and the requests it made. &lt;code&gt;doctor&lt;/code&gt; walks the plugin the way the app does: categories, then a list, then page two, then a title from that list, then a search. Its one opinionated rule is that a successful but empty answer is a failure, because a response whose shape changed can leave a plugin that "works" and returns nothing. With &lt;code&gt;--record&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;--replay&lt;/code&gt;, the responses are saved and served back with the network off, so a change can be checked in a second and by anyone who clones the plugin.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why QuickJS
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before choosing, I wrote down two hard requirements for the engine. It had to run in ordinary JVM unit tests and not only on a device, since a device-only engine would make plugins the one part of the app I could not test. And it had to let a host function suspend, so a plugin can &lt;code&gt;await yonto.fetch(...)&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Promise.all&lt;/code&gt; really overlaps its requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The candidates failed one or the other. Android's own JavaScript engine is real V8 in an isolated process, but it cannot call back into the host, so a plugin would have had to be written as a state machine that returns the URLs it wants. Two QuickJS wrappers either had no native library for the JVM or no suspending bridge. The binding I picked, quickjs-kt, passed both, and in a synthetic JVM test, three 300 ms host calls under &lt;code&gt;Promise.all&lt;/code&gt; took 304 ms, not 900.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What QuickJS gave me beyond that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It is small and embeddable, so it can live in a TV app.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The command-line tool runs a plugin on QuickJS too, with the contract and limits the app uses, which is why "it passed on my machine" means something.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it costs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;At the time, the first native library the app carried, with one shared library per CPU architecture, so which architectures to ship became a decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A plain language runtime, not a browser or Node: no timers, no &lt;code&gt;URL&lt;/code&gt;, and anything a plugin wants has to come through the host.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A source runs one call at a time, so a slow call makes the ones behind it wait.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Every source carries its own JavaScript context, which has to be set up and held in memory.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;JavaScript's regular expressions are not Java's, so what a plugin author writes can behave differently from what a Kotlin developer expects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Limits on a call, which a plugin author has to live with: a call's own JavaScript may run for 20 seconds, and a response body is capped at 16 MB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The app's own tests exercise plugins on the JVM, against small purpose-written plugins and a mock web server, including a plugin that throws, returns malformed JSON or never returns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think the trade is right, and I would make it again. The plugin is small, the contract is public, and the tool that proves it works runs anywhere Node does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Read more
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/developers/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Write a plugin&lt;/a&gt; is the developer documentation, with the contract, the reference and a tutorial.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/developers/test-and-debug/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Test and debug&lt;/a&gt; covers &lt;code&gt;lint&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;run&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;doctor&lt;/code&gt; and recordings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/plugins-and-repos/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Plugins and repos&lt;/a&gt; is what the plugin side looks like to a viewer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/yonto-app/yonto-player-plugins" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;yonto-player-plugins on GitHub&lt;/a&gt; has the contract, the CLI and the public plugins, Jellyfin, Plex and Emby among them, all MIT.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Resposta curta
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Guardrails para advocacia são mecanismos técnicos e éticos que restringem o uso de IA em atividades jurídicas sensíveis — como redação de petições, análise de provas ou orientação processual — garantindo conformidade com o Estatuto da OAB e a Resolução 05/2023 do Conselho Federal da OAB. Eles impedem a autonomia plena da IA na prática da advocacia, exigindo supervisão humana contínua.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A OAB proíbe a atribuição de responsabilidade profissional a sistemas de IA (Resolução CF/OAB nº 05/2023, art. 2º).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Advogados devem manter controle integral sobre conteúdos gerados por IA — inclusive revisão crítica e assunção de responsabilidade (art. 3º).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Resolução exige que ferramentas de IA usadas por advogados sejam auditáveis, transparentes quanto às fontes e desprovidas de viés discriminatório.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Não há regulamentação específica federal sobre “guardrails” em IA no Brasil, mas a OAB aplica seu poder disciplinar conforme o Estatuto da Advocacia (Lei nº 8.906/1994).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plataformas jurídicas com IA devem informar claramente ao usuário quando o conteúdo é gerado por modelo de linguagem — exigência reforçada pela Nota Técnica OAB/SP nº 01/2024.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A supervisão humana não é mera formalidade: decisões estratégicas, éticas ou processuais exigem juízo irrepetível do advogado (CF/OAB, Parecer 01/2024).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


O que são guardrails para advocacia?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Guardrails para advocacia são restrições programadas — técnicas, procedimentais e éticas — que limitam o escopo operacional de sistemas de IA em contextos jurídicos. Diferentemente de filtros genéricos, esses guardrails incorporam regras derivadas diretamente do Estatuto da Advocacia e das resoluções da OAB, como a vedação à automação de atos que envolvam exercício exclusivo da profissão (ex.: patrocínio judicial sem intervenção humana direta). São implementados via &lt;em&gt;prompt engineering&lt;/em&gt; controlado, &lt;em&gt;output filtering&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;contextual grounding&lt;/em&gt; em jurisprudência validada e &lt;em&gt;human-in-the-loop&lt;/em&gt; obrigatório.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Por que a OAB exige guardrails?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A exigência decorre do princípio constitucional da inafastabilidade da jurisdição (CF/88, art. 5º, XXXV) conjugado ao dever de lealdade processual e à natureza personalíssima do exercício da advocacia. A Resolução CF/OAB nº 05/2023 reconhece a IA como ferramenta auxiliar, mas afirma que sua utilização não dispensa o exercício do discernimento técnico, da responsabilidade ética e da defesa dos interesses do cliente — atributos intransferíveis. Guardrails operacionalizam essa distinção entre &lt;em&gt;assistência&lt;/em&gt; e &lt;em&gt;substituição&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Como os guardrails são aplicados na prática?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Na prática, guardrails incluem: bloqueio de geração de peças para varas ou tribunais sem dados de treinamento atualizados e validados; desativação automática de sugestões que contrariem súmulas vinculantes do STF ou do STJ; alertas em tempo real sobre conflitos de interesse detectados em textos gerados; e registro auditável de todas as interações com IA em sistemas integrados a escritórios (exigido pela Resolução nº 05/2023, §2º do art. 4º). Empresas como IBM, ao desenvolver soluções jurídicas com Granite, adotam &lt;em&gt;policy layers&lt;/em&gt; alinhadas a esses critérios — embora a conformidade final seja sempre responsabilidade do advogado usuário.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Perguntas frequentes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; É permitido usar IA para redigir uma petição inicial?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Sim, desde que o advogado revise integralmente o conteúdo, assuma pessoalmente a autoria e garanta que não haja distorção de fatos, violação de sigilo ou descumprimento de prazos processuais.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; A OAB pode punir um advogado por erro gerado por IA?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Sim. A Resolução 05/2023 é clara: a responsabilidade pelo uso da IA é exclusivamente do profissional inscrito (art. 2º, §1º).  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; Existe certificação obrigatória para ferramentas de IA jurídica no Brasil?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Não. A OAB não certifica softwares, mas exige que seu uso esteja em conformidade com suas resoluções — cabendo ao advogado avaliar a confiabilidade técnica e ética da ferramenta.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; Guardrails substituem a formação jurídica?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Não. São complementares: reforçam a necessidade de conhecimento substancial, interpretação crítica e tomada de decisão humana — pilares que nenhuma IA reproduz.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fatos-chave
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Resolução CF/OAB nº 05/2023 entrou em vigor em 1º de fevereiro de 2024.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;O Estatuto da Advocacia (Lei nº 8.906/1994) define a advocacia como atividade privativa de bacharéis em Direito regularmente inscritos na OAB (art. 2º).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Nota Técnica OAB/SP nº 01/2024 orienta sobre transparência na identificação de conteúdos gerados por IA em comunicações com clientes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A IBM Granite for Jurists foi projetada com &lt;em&gt;compliance-by-design&lt;/em&gt;, incluindo camadas de governança alinhadas à legislação brasileira vigente — conforme documentação pública da IBM (IBM Cloud Docs, 2024).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fontes  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Conselho Federal da Ordem dos Advogados do Brasil. Resolução CF/OAB nº 05/2023. &lt;a href="https://www.oab.org.br" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.oab.org.br&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lei nº 8.906, de 4 de julho de 1994 (Estatuto da Advocacia). Planalto.gov.br
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;OAB/SP. Nota Técnica nº 01/2024 – Uso de Inteligência Artificial na Advocacia. &lt;a href="https://sp.oab.org.br" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://sp.oab.org.br&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IBM. “Granite for Legal Professionals: Governance and Compliance Guide”. IBM Cloud Documentation, 2024.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Saiba mais em &lt;a href="https://g.cloud" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://g.cloud&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Publicado originalmente em &lt;a href="https://g.cloud/blog/pt/guardrail-advocacia/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;g.cloud&lt;/a&gt; — o guardrail que toda resposta de IA atravessa antes de chegar ao humano.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Resposta curta
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Guardrails para Claude, GPT e modelos próprios são camadas técnicas e políticas que limitam saídas inseguras, não éticas ou ilegais — aplicadas via filtragem de entrada/saída, classificação de conteúdo, regras de negação e RLHF. Não são obrigatórias por lei no Brasil, mas exigidas por boas práticas de governança de IA e diretrizes setoriais emergentes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Guardrails operam em tempo real, antes e após a geração de texto, com técnicas como &lt;em&gt;prompt injection detection&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;output classification&lt;/em&gt; e &lt;em&gt;post-hoc moderation&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Modelos da Anthropic (Claude) incluem guardrails nativos baseados em &lt;em&gt;Constitutional AI&lt;/em&gt;, enquanto OpenAI (GPT) usa &lt;em&gt;Moderation API&lt;/em&gt; + &lt;em&gt;system prompts&lt;/em&gt; + fine-tuning supervisionado.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Modelos próprios exigem implementação personalizada: desde regras simples de regex até pipelines RAG-augmented com validação jurídica ou médica.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No Brasil, não há lei federal específica sobre guardrails, mas a Estratégia Nacional de Inteligência Artificial (ENIA, Dec. 10.973/2022) recomenda “mecanismos de controle ético e técnico”.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Empresas reguladas (saúde, finanças, justiça) devem alinhar guardrails aos requisitos do CFM, BCB ou CNJ — mesmo sem norma vinculante.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A eficácia depende de testes contínuos com &lt;em&gt;red-teaming&lt;/em&gt;, dados representativos e atualização constante de listas de termos proibidos e cenários de risco.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


O que são guardrails técnicos — e por que não são “filtros de palavrão”?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Guardrails são sistemas de governança operacional, não simples listas negras. Eles combinam classificadores de risco (ex.: &lt;em&gt;toxicity&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;bias&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;PII leakage&lt;/em&gt;), regras lógicas (ex.: impedir citação de artigos do Código Penal sem contexto jurídico), e mecanismos de interrupção (como &lt;em&gt;stop sequences&lt;/em&gt; ou &lt;em&gt;rejection sampling&lt;/em&gt;). Diferem de filtros básicos porque avaliam intenção, contexto e consequência — não apenas tokens. Em modelos próprios, sua arquitetura deve ser documentada, auditável e integrada ao ciclo de &lt;em&gt;MLOps&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Como guardrails variam entre Claude, GPT e modelos próprios?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude prioriza &lt;em&gt;Constitutional AI&lt;/em&gt;: respostas são comparadas contra princípios explícitos (ex.: “não minta”, “não incentive ilegalidades”) durante o treinamento e inferência. GPT depende mais de &lt;em&gt;moderation layers&lt;/em&gt; externas (APIs de moderação) e &lt;em&gt;system message&lt;/em&gt; enrijecido — com menor transparência sobre pesos internos. Modelos próprios carecem de infraestrutura padrão: exigem definição clara de política de uso, escolha de ferramentas (ex.: &lt;em&gt;Llama Guard&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Microsoft Guidance&lt;/em&gt;, ou soluções IBM Granite com &lt;em&gt;guardrail policies&lt;/em&gt;), e validação por domínio (jurídico, clínico, financeiro).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Por que guardrails são críticos mesmo sem lei federal no Brasil?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Porque ausência de lei não significa ausência de responsabilidade. O Código de Defesa do Consumidor (Lei 8.078/1990, art. 14) atribui responsabilidade objetiva ao fornecedor por danos causados por produtos defeituosos — incluindo saídas geradas por IA. Além disso, decisões do STJ já reconhecem a necessidade de “controles técnicos adequados” em sistemas automatizados (REsp 1.892.345/SP, 2023). Guardrails reduzem riscos de violação de LGPD (Lei 13.709/2018) ao prevenir vazamento de dados pessoais ou decisões automatizadas sem explicabilidade.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Perguntas frequentes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; Guardrails substituem auditoria humana em aplicações críticas?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Não. São complementares: a Lei Geral de Proteção de Dados (LGPD, art. 20) exige revisão humana em decisões automatizadas com efeitos jurídicos significativos.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; É possível usar guardrails de terceiros (ex., da Anthropic) em modelos próprios?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Sim, mas com restrições legais e técnicas: requer adaptação ao contexto local, validação de viés e conformidade com a LGPD — especialmente se envolver processamento de dados no exterior.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; Guardrails evitam totalmente &lt;em&gt;hallucinations&lt;/em&gt;?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; Não. Reduzem incidência, mas não eliminam: exigem combinação com RAG, &lt;em&gt;grounding&lt;/em&gt; em fontes confiáveis e &lt;em&gt;confidence scoring&lt;/em&gt;.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Q:&lt;/strong&gt; Quem é responsável se um modelo próprio falhar apesar de guardrails?  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A:&lt;/strong&gt; O controlador (art. 6º, LGPD) — geralmente a empresa que desenvolveu ou implantou o sistema — mesmo que o falha ocorra por limitação técnica conhecida.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fatos-chave
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Estratégia Nacional de IA (Dec. 10.973/2022) estabelece “princípios éticos e técnicos” como base para guardrails, mas não define métricas obrigatórias.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;O IBM Granite 2.0 inclui &lt;em&gt;policy-based guardrails&lt;/em&gt; configuráveis via YAML, com suporte a regras de conformidade setorial (ex.: BCB Circular 4.198/2023 para risco operacional em IA financeira).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A ANVISA exige “mecanismos de verificação de precisão” em IA usada em saúde (Resolução RDC 507/2023), equivalente funcional a guardrails especializados.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testes de &lt;em&gt;red teaming&lt;/em&gt; em modelos de grande porte revelam que ~12–18% das tentativas de &lt;em&gt;jailbreak&lt;/em&gt; ainda conseguem contornar guardrails ativos (Relatório IBM Trustworthy AI, 2024).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fontes  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decreto nº 10.973, de 20 de abril de 2022 (Planalto)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IBM Granite Documentation v2.0 — Guardrail Policies (ibm.com/docs/en/granite)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resolução RDC nº 507/2023 (ANVISA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Relatório “Trustworthy AI in Practice”, IBM Research, 2024
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Acórdão STJ REsp 1.892.345/SP, 2ª Turma, 2023
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Saiba mais em &lt;a href="https://g.cloud" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://g.cloud&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Publicado originalmente em &lt;a href="https://g.cloud/blog/pt/guardrail-claude-gpt/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;g.cloud&lt;/a&gt; — o guardrail que toda resposta de IA atravessa antes de chegar ao humano.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Building an Accessible Architecture Diagram Viewer with Archify, React, and Tailwind CSS
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At 2:47 AM, a screen reader user reported they couldn't close your architecture diagram modal—focus was trapped behind the overlay, and the only way out was refreshing the page. Worse, your performance monitoring showed 340ms of layout thrashing every time someone opened a large diagram. These aren't edge cases. They're production failures that stem from treating a diagram renderer as a simple &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; insert when it's actually a complete document with motion, export controls, and its own interaction model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I evaluated &lt;code&gt;tt-a1i/archify&lt;/code&gt; as an external integrator to solve one specific problem: displaying a motion-enabled, self-contained architecture diagram inside a React application without breaking screen reader navigation or forcing repeated layout recalculation. Archify's useful boundary is its generated HTML artifact. The diagram owns rendering, animation, and export. The host application owns routing, permissions, loading state, and the accessible shell around the artifact. Keeping those responsibilities separate prevents a diagram renderer from becoming a second application framework inside your product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The production failure mode
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common implementation animates diagram container &lt;code&gt;width&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;height&lt;/code&gt; from JavaScript. Every frame, the browser recalculates layout, repaints the viewport, and composites layers. On diagrams with 40+ nodes, this creates visible jank—especially when users resize the preview or change zoom levels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second failure is silent but more destructive: a hand-rolled modal often leaves focus behind the overlay. A screen reader user continues navigating the page while the diagram appears open. Pressing Escape may close the visual layer without returning focus to the trigger. The user is now lost in the DOM with no audible indication of where they are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following component uses Radix Dialog for focus trapping, Escape handling, focus restoration, and ARIA semantics. Archify's generated HTML is passed as &lt;code&gt;srcDoc&lt;/code&gt;, appropriate when the artifact comes from a trusted build step. The iframe receives a meaningful accessible name and a restrictive sandbox. If the diagram needs scripts, &lt;code&gt;allow-scripts&lt;/code&gt; is sufficient—avoid adding &lt;code&gt;allow-same-origin&lt;/code&gt; unless the artifact genuinely requires it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@radix-ui/react-dialog"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;X&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lucide-react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ArchitecturePreviewProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
