&lt;p&gt;System design estimation is a way to turn an ambiguous product description into approximate traffic, storage, bandwidth, and capacity requirements. The goal is not to predict an exact production number. It is to expose the order of magnitude, make assumptions visible, and identify which part of the design deserves attention first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful estimate follows a repeatable sequence: define the workload, calculate average and peak requests per second, split reads from writes, estimate data growth, calculate network throughput, and translate the results into design choices. The arithmetic should be simple enough to explain aloud and detailed enough for another engineer to challenge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; Treat every number in the worked examples as an explicit hypothetical input, not a benchmark or a universal industry default. Replace assumptions with measured product analytics, load tests, payload samples, and retention requirements when sizing a real system.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The estimation method: assumptions before formulas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start by describing what the system does. “A social feed” is not a workload; “two million daily active users each request twenty feed pages and publish half a post per day on average” is a workload you can calculate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Write down the assumptions before doing arithmetic:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Users and activity:&lt;/strong&gt; daily active users (DAU), actions per user, and whether actions happen evenly or in bursts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Operation mix:&lt;/strong&gt; reads, writes, uploads, downloads, background jobs, and internal fan-out.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payload sizes:&lt;/strong&gt; average request, response, record, and file sizes. Keep ingress and egress separate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Retention:&lt;/strong&gt; how long records, events, files, and derived data remain available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traffic shape:&lt;/strong&gt; observed peak-to-average ratio, seasonal events, regional time zones, and retry behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Capacity target:&lt;/strong&gt; the utilization or headroom the system should retain during normal and degraded operation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask which values are known, which are estimates, and which are deliberately conservative. If a prompt does not provide a value, choose a round assumption, say why it is plausible for the scenario, and show how the result changes if it is wrong. In an interview, this is part of the answer—not a detour before the answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The conversions worth keeping close
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A day contains 86,400 seconds. For quick mental arithmetic, 100,000 seconds is a convenient approximation, but use 86,400 when showing a worked calculation. The approximation makes the result about 13.6% lower than the exact division, so it is useful for a first pass, not a substitute for checking a decision near a capacity boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Time interval&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Seconds&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1 day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;86,400&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;30-day month&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2,592,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;365-day year&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;31,536,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For traffic, remember the order of magnitude: one million requests per day is 1,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = 11.57 requests per second, or roughly 12 average requests per second. Ten million per day is about 116/s; one hundred million per day is about 1,157/s.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be precise about data units. Decimal units use powers of 10: 1 MB = 1,000,000 bytes and 1 GB = 1,000,000,000 bytes. Binary units use powers of 2: 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes, 1 MiB = 1,048,576 bytes, and 1 GiB = 1,073,741,824 bytes. NIST documents the distinction between SI and IEC binary prefixes. &lt;a href="https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/Units/binary.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;NIST: Definitions of the SI units — binary prefixes&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use one convention throughout a calculation. In the examples below, storage totals use decimal GB and TB; file sizes use MiB where binary units are more natural. State the convention rather than switching between MB and MiB as if they were identical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Core formulas for system design estimation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These formulas provide a starting model. They do not capture every system cost, but they expose the main quantities that influence an initial architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Average and peak request rate
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;requests_per_day = DAU × requests_per_user_per_day
average_RPS      = requests_per_day ÷ 86,400
peak_RPS         = average_RPS × peak_to_average_factor
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;RPS means requests per second. QPS means queries per second; teams sometimes use the terms interchangeably, but define whether a “request” is an external API call, a database query, or an internal operation. One external request can fan out into several internal calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not assume one peak factor fits every product. Calculate it from observed time-bucketed traffic when possible: peak interval rate divided by the average rate for the same interval definition. If no telemetry exists, state a scenario assumption and test alternatives. A factor of 3 in an example means “we are modeling a peak three times the average,” not “all systems peak at 3×.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calculate read and write rates separately. A read-heavy product may need to serve a large volume of repeated data, while a write-heavy product may need sustained ingestion, ordering, durability, or downstream processing. A combined RPS figure can hide the real constraint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Storage growth
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;new_data_per_day = writes_per_day × average_record_size
logical_storage  = new_data_per_day × retention_days
physical_storage = logical_storage × copy_factor
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add indexes, metadata, compaction space, snapshots, backups, and derived data separately. A copy factor is a simplified planning input, not a universal statement about how a managed database or object store implements durability. Compression also changes the stored size; estimate it from representative data rather than assuming a fixed saving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Network bandwidth
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;bytes_per_second = requests_per_second × average_payload_bytes
bits_per_second  = bytes_per_second × 8
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Calculate ingress and egress independently. Use the relevant request or response size for each path. Add protocol overhead, encryption, retries, fan-out, and retransmission where they materially affect the estimate. For large files, a small request count can still produce substantial bandwidth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Server count
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;required_instances = ceil(peak_RPS ÷ sustainable_RPS_per_instance)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the capacity target is below full utilization, use &lt;code&gt;ceil(peak_RPS ÷ (sustainable_RPS_per_instance × target_utilization))&lt;/code&gt;. The per-instance rate must come from a representative load test or a defensible capacity measurement for the actual workload, software, instance type, and latency target. Do not copy a generic “requests per server” number into a design and present it as fact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Worked example 1: a read-heavy feed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assume a feed service has 2 million daily active users. Each user loads twenty feed pages per day and creates half a post per day on average. Model peak traffic at four times the daily average. Assume each post record occupies 1,000 bytes and each feed response transfers 12,000 bytes. These are illustrative inputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Calculate reads.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;reads/day = 2,000,000 users × 20 reads = 40,000,000
average read RPS = 40,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = 462.96 ≈ 463
peak read RPS = 463 × 4 ≈ 1,852
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Calculate writes.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;posts/day = 2,000,000 × 0.5 = 1,000,000
average write RPS = 1,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = 11.57 ≈ 11.6
peak write RPS = 11.57 × 4 = 46.3
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The workload is roughly 40 reads for every write. That ratio does not prove a cache is required, but it tells you the read path deserves more attention than the write path in the first design pass.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Estimate response bandwidth.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;peak egress = 1,852 responses/s × 12,000 bytes
= 22,224,000 bytes/s
≈ 22.2 MB/s ≈ 178 Mbps
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is application response payload only. It excludes transport overhead and any media served separately. If feed responses include images, model image delivery as a separate path rather than hiding it inside an average API response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Estimate post storage.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;new logical data/day = 1,000,000 posts × 1,000 bytes = 1 GB
one year              = 1 GB/day × 365 = 365 GB
three-copy scenario   = 365 GB × 3 = 1.095 TB
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The 1.095 TB figure excludes indexes, metadata, backups, and media. It is not a complete database capacity plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Design implications:&lt;/strong&gt; investigate caching or precomputing frequently requested feed data, and consider read replicas only if measured database read pressure justifies them. Keep writes on a simpler path initially unless ordering, fan-out, or consistency requirements change the picture. Separate media delivery from feed API responses, and validate the assumed read distribution: a highly personalized feed may not benefit from the same cache strategy as a feed of mostly shared content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Worked example 2: a write-heavy event log
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assume 500,000 daily active devices each emit 200 events per day. Each stored event, including its basic metadata, averages 600 bytes. Model peak ingestion at five times the average and retain events for 30 days. Use a three-copy scenario for a first storage estimate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Calculate event volume and ingestion rate.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;events/day = 500,000 × 200 = 100,000,000
average write RPS = 100,000,000 ÷ 86,400 = 1,157.4
peak write RPS = 1,157.4 × 5 = 5,787
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Calculate storage.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;logical data/day = 100,000,000 × 600 bytes = 60,000,000,000 bytes
= 60 GB/day
30-day logical storage = 60 GB × 30 = 1.8 TB
three-copy scenario = 1.8 TB × 3 = 5.4 TB
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The 5.4 TB estimate excludes indexes, compression effects, retention gaps, backups, and temporary compaction space. If event payloads vary substantially, calculate by event class or use a measured size distribution instead of relying on one average.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Check ingress bandwidth.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;peak ingress = 5,787 events/s × 600 bytes
= 3,472,200 bytes/s
≈ 3.47 MB/s ≈ 27.8 Mbps
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The request rate is more revealing than the raw bandwidth in this example. The system must accept and durably process nearly 5,800 small events per second at the modeled peak, even though the byte rate is modest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Design implications:&lt;/strong&gt; consider an append-oriented ingestion path, batching, and a durable queue or log when producers and consumers need decoupling. Partitioning can distribute sustained write volume, but the partition key must avoid concentrating one hot tenant or event type on a single partition. Define ordering requirements before choosing a partition strategy: global ordering is much more restrictive than per-device or per-aggregate ordering. Use retention and compaction policies that match the actual query and audit requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Worked example 3: a file upload service
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assume 100,000 daily active users upload two files each per day. The average file is 8 MiB. Model upload peaks at eight times the average. Assume each uploaded file is downloaded three times on average, downloads peak at four times their average, and files are retained for 30 days. For a simplified capacity scenario, count three stored copies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Calculate upload traffic.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;uploads/day = 100,000 × 2 = 200,000
average upload RPS = 200,000 ÷ 86,400 = 2.31
peak upload RPS = 2.31 × 8 = 18.52
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The request rate looks small. The payload size changes the capacity picture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Calculate upload bandwidth.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;peak ingress = 18.52 uploads/s × 8 MiB
≈ 155.3 MB/s ≈ 1.24 Gbps
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The conversion uses 8 MiB = 8,388,608 bytes and decimal MB for the final rate. This is a payload estimate, not a provisioned network target; protocol overhead and burst shape still matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Calculate storage.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;data/day = 200,000 × 8,388,608 bytes
= 1.678 TB/day (decimal)
30-day logical storage = 1.678 TB × 30 ≈ 50.33 TB
three-copy scenario = 50.33 TB × 3 ≈ 150.99 TB
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Estimate download egress.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;downloads/day = 200,000 uploads × 3 = 600,000
average download RPS = 600,000 ÷ 86,400 = 6.94
peak download RPS = 6.94 × 4 = 27.78
peak egress = 27.78 × 8 MiB ≈ 233 MB/s ≈ 1.86 Gbps
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Design implications:&lt;/strong&gt; this is a data-transfer and storage problem, not simply an API request-count problem. Consider direct-to-object-storage uploads using short-lived, scoped upload authorization so application servers do not proxy every file byte. Use asynchronous processing for malware scanning, metadata extraction, and thumbnail generation when those tasks do not need to block the upload response. A CDN may reduce origin egress for repeatedly downloaded files, subject to access control and cache policy. Confirm whether “three copies” is actually how the chosen storage service bills or exposes durability; do not multiply managed storage by three if its documented storage model already accounts for redundancy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Compare the three estimates
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Example&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Peak primary operation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Data growth&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Main pressure to investigate&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read-heavy feed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~1,852 reads/s; ~46 writes/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1 GB/day logical posts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read path, cacheability, feed fan-out&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Write-heavy event log&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~5,787 writes/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;60 GB/day logical events&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sustained ingestion, partitioning, retention&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;File upload service&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~18.5 uploads/s; ~27.8 downloads/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~1.68 TB/day logical files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ingress/egress bandwidth and object storage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The examples are not directly comparable capacity benchmarks: their workloads, payloads, and assumptions differ. Their purpose is to show how the same method reveals different constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sanity checks before you trust the result
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run these checks before turning estimates into an architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Recalculate from the other direction.&lt;/strong&gt; Multiply average RPS by 86,400 and confirm that it returns the daily request count within rounding error.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check units at every step.&lt;/strong&gt; Requests/s × bytes/request gives bytes/s. Multiply by eight only when converting bytes to bits. Do not label MB/s as Mbps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Separate external and internal work.&lt;/strong&gt; If one request causes five database queries or three downstream calls, estimate those operations separately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check peak and average consistency.&lt;/strong&gt; Apply a peak factor to the same time unit and traffic category. Do not multiply a peak rate by a daily count again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test the assumptions.&lt;/strong&gt; Recalculate with half and twice the assumed activity, payload size, or retention. If the design changes sharply, the assumption needs measurement.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep logical and physical storage distinct.&lt;/strong&gt; State whether the estimate includes copies, indexes, backups, compression, and derived data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Look for missing workload dimensions.&lt;/strong&gt; Concurrent connections, background jobs, retries, batch windows, and hot-key skew can matter even when average RPS looks modest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Turn estimates into design decisions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An estimate is useful only when it changes a question or decision. Use the numbers to identify what to investigate—not to select a database or cache by formula alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Caching:&lt;/strong&gt; consider it when reads repeat, data has an acceptable freshness window, and measured origin work is expensive. A high read/write ratio is a clue, not proof that every response is cacheable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Replication:&lt;/strong&gt; separate read scaling, availability, and durability requirements. Replicas add operational and consistency considerations; they are not a universal response to high RPS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Partitioning:&lt;/strong&gt; investigate it when write volume, storage growth, or data ownership exceeds a single partition's tested limits. Choose a key based on access patterns and skew, not only on even-looking IDs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Queues and logs:&lt;/strong&gt; use them when ingestion and processing need independent scaling, buffering, replay, or failure isolation. They add delivery semantics and operational complexity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CDNs and object storage:&lt;/strong&gt; consider them when large or repeated file transfers dominate bandwidth or application-server work. Access control and cache invalidation remain part of the design.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Capacity and headroom:&lt;/strong&gt; use representative load tests to estimate sustainable per-instance throughput at the latency and error-rate targets that matter. Then calculate instance count with the formula above and add failure or deployment headroom explicitly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an interview, narrate the assumptions, show the arithmetic, identify the largest constraint, and explain what you would measure next. For a real design, record the inputs, source, confidence, and owner beside the estimate. A useful next step is to put the assumptions and capacity implications into a &lt;a href="https://letinix.com/resources/technical-design-doc-template" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;technical design document&lt;/a&gt;, where reviewers can challenge the numbers before they become infrastructure commitments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the &lt;a href="https://letinix.com/resources/system-design-cheat-sheet" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;System Design Cheat Sheet&lt;/a&gt; as the companion reference while practicing these calculations. The cheat sheet is the primary resource for this topic; this article explains the reasoning behind the estimates and how to connect them to architectural choices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://pml.nist.gov/cuu/Units/binary.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Definitions of the SI units: The binary prefixes — National Institute of Standards and Technology&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on &lt;a href="https://letinix.com/articles/engineer-growth/back-of-the-envelope-system-design-estimation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Letinix&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 7 November 2026 I added analytics to my store and rewarded myself by breaking the entire site's text. Every word on every page rendered as garbled symbols in my browser. The build was fine. The files were fine. The words were fine, somewhere underneath whatever my browser had decided they were.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site is a small store built with Astro, deployed on Vercel. The change that morning was tiny and boring: install Vercel Analytics, drop the component into the base layout, deploy. The kind of change you make between sips of tea.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I opened the store and it looked like the text had been fed through a paper shredder and reassembled by an enemy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I checked first (all wrong)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I checked the content files. Clean. I checked the build output locally. Clean. I rebuilt. I stared at fonts, because garbled text feels like a font problem the way a cough feels like a lung problem. It usually isn't, but the feeling is strong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is what I had not checked: the actual bytes of the page my browser received.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The analytics component injects an inline script into the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. My layout had the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;meta charset&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tag sitting after other elements, and the injected script landed before it. In the served HTML, the charset declaration ended up at byte offset 3,410.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That number is the whole story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why 3,410 bytes is a death sentence for a charset tag
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a server sends an HTML page without declaring the character encoding in the &lt;code&gt;Content-Type&lt;/code&gt; response header, the browser has to guess. The HTML spec gives it a prescan step: it reads the beginning of the byte stream looking for a charset declaration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is the size of that beginning. The prescan only looks at roughly the first 1,024 bytes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My charset tag was at byte 3,410. From the browser's point of view, it did not exist. So the browser fell back to a guessed encoding, guessed wrong, and rendered UTF-8 text through the wrong lens. Every multi-byte character smeared into symbols. The page was never broken. It was being read in the wrong language by a reader following its own rules correctly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And my response headers carried no charset either, so there was no second chance. The meta tag was the only witness, and it was standing 3,410 bytes away from the only place witnesses get heard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The fix, which is embarrassing in its size
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Move the charset meta to be the first element inside &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Before the title. Before everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One line, relocated. Redeploy. In the new served HTML the charset sat at byte offset 44, comfortably inside the prescan window, and the store read like a store again. The checkout page returned 200, the text was clean, done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Total code changed: the position of one tag. Total time spent suspecting my content, my fonts and my build before looking at the byte offsets: considerably longer, and I'd rather not be precise about it, which tells you everything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this one cost me in dignity, and what it taught me
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Encoding bugs wear a perfect disguise. They look exactly like content bugs. The words are what's visibly wrong, so you interrogate the words, and the words are innocent, and they stay innocent no matter how many times you rebuild them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The debugging rule I now follow, learned at byte 3,410: when text renders wrong, check how the bytes are being read before checking what the bytes say. View source. Count where the charset declaration actually sits in the served document, not where you placed it in your template, because frameworks, injectors and "helpful" components rearrange furniture in your &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; without asking. My template had the tag in a perfectly reasonable position. The served page had it somewhere else entirely. The template is a promise; the served bytes are the truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This applies to anything that injects into your head section: analytics, tag managers, A/B testing scripts, consent banners. Every one of them pushes your own tags further from byte zero. Most of the time it costs nothing. The day your headers don't declare a charset, it costs you your whole site's legibility, and the bill arrives looking like a content problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Your smallest cause?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm collecting these now, the bugs where the cause-to-symptom ratio is most insulting. Mine: one misplaced tag, 2,386 bytes past the limit, every word on a live store unreadable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's yours? The one-character typo that ate a weekend, the config line that took down the thing it was supposed to protect. Smallest cause, biggest symptom. I want to feel better, and also worse.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;P.S. Everything I write stays free — that part doesn't change. But if this one was worth something to you, you can buy me a coffee ☕ (one-time or monthly, both work). It covers the API credits and hosting behind these experiments — and honestly, it's also the nudge that gets the next one started on the evenings I'd otherwise skip it. — Salam&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.patreon.com/c/AheadKit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F87bycvv0drlpbwxjppyi.png" alt="Buy me a coffee" width="800" height="192"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most React tutorials stop at the same place: &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, props, maybe context. Then they stop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The real questions come after that. Why does my component re-render? What happens during the commit phase? How does hydration work? What is a custom renderer?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I found a curriculum that answers those. It's on &lt;strong&gt;KandZ Tuts&lt;/strong&gt;, and it's 131 chapters, organized as a single progression from your first component to React's internal architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the breakdown.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Foundations (1–14)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What React is, Node.js and package managers, creating a project with Vite, project structure, JSX syntax and rules, embedding JavaScript expressions, functional components, importing and exporting, props, default props, the &lt;code&gt;children&lt;/code&gt; prop, event listeners, passing handlers as props, and the synthetic event object.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


State (15–24)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;, updating primitive state, updating state from previous state, updating objects and arrays in state, conditional rendering with &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt;/ternary/logical AND, rendering lists with &lt;code&gt;map&lt;/code&gt;, and the importance of keys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Forms (25–29)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Controlled inputs, textareas and selects, handling multiple inputs with one handler, form submission and &lt;code&gt;preventDefault&lt;/code&gt;, and uncontrolled inputs with &lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Effects and Data (30–34)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, dependency array rules, cleanup functions, fetching API data, and handling loading and error states.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Custom Hooks and Context (35–41)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Extracting reusable logic, the prop drilling problem, &lt;code&gt;createContext&lt;/code&gt;, providing and consuming context, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;, and combining context with &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Refs, Memoization, and Modern Hooks (42–57)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt; for DOM access and mutable values, &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useImperativeHandle&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useDebugValue&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useTransition&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useDeferredValue&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useId&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useSyncExternalStore&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useOptimistic&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useActionState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useFormStatus&lt;/code&gt;, and the &lt;code&gt;use&lt;/code&gt; hook.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Routing and State Libraries (58–70)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Router (route elements, outlets, dynamic params, programmatic navigation, protected and nested routes), TanStack Query (setup, &lt;code&gt;useQuery&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useMutation&lt;/code&gt;, cache invalidation), Redux Toolkit (provider, slices, actions, reducers, middleware, thunks), and Zustand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Styling and Forms (71–75)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CSS Modules, Tailwind CSS, styled-components and Emotion, React Hook Form, and Zod integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Advanced Patterns (76–87)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code splitting with &lt;code&gt;React.lazy&lt;/code&gt;, Suspense, error boundaries, render props, higher-order components, compound components, control props, portals, React Testing Library, user interaction testing, MSW, and Playwright.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


SSR and Frameworks (88–95)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SSR vs SSG, React Server Components architecture, Server Components vs Client Components rules, Server Actions, Next.js App Router, layouts and loading UI, streaming SSR and progressive hydration, and micro-frontends with Module Federation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Production and Accessibility (96–100)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation and focus management, XSS prevention, React DevTools profiling, and production builds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


React Internals (101–109)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fiber architecture and reconciliation, the commit phase vs render phase, double-pass rendering and Strict Mode, the concurrent rendering scheduler and priorities, automatic batching, memory leak prevention, synthetic event pooling and delegation, hydration mismatch errors, and progressive and selective hydration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Advanced Frameworks (110–114)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SSG with React frameworks, Incremental Static Regeneration, Partial Prerender, React Server Functions and the wire format, and the Taint APIs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Applied React (115–120)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;i18n and localization, XState state machines, WebSockets, offline-first apps with service workers, monorepos with Turborepo and Nx, and building reusable component libraries with Storybook.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Beyond the Browser (121–130)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React Native core, React Native navigation and native modules, Electron and Tauri, React Three Fiber, WebGL and Canvas, React Konva, the Web Audio API, Web Components integration, custom Babel/SWC plugins, and custom React renderers with &lt;code&gt;react-reconciler&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Chapter 131: The Complete Cheatsheet
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why the Internals Section Matters
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chapters 101–109 are the reason to read this curriculum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most resources stop at "here's how &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; works." This one goes into &lt;strong&gt;how React actually renders&lt;/strong&gt;—Fiber, reconciliation, the commit phase, the concurrent scheduler, automatic batching, hydration mismatches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's what lets you diagnose a performance problem instead of guessing at &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;. It's the difference between using React and understanding it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The &lt;code&gt;use&lt;/code&gt; Hook and React 19
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chapters 54–57 cover the hooks that define modern React: &lt;code&gt;useOptimistic&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useActionState&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useFormStatus&lt;/code&gt;, and the &lt;code&gt;use&lt;/code&gt; hook.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are not footnotes. They are how React 19 handles async forms, optimistic updates, and reading promises in render. A curriculum that skips them is teaching React from two years ago.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Beyond the Browser
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chapters 121–130 are unusual. Most React curricula stop at the web. This one covers React Native, Electron, Tauri, React Three Fiber, React Konva, the Web Audio API, and custom renderers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've wondered how React renders to a canvas, a native mobile view, or a 3D scene, this is where that gets explained.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Who This Is For
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Beginners:&lt;/strong&gt; Start at Chapter 1 and read in order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Experienced React developers:&lt;/strong&gt; Jump to Chapter 57 (the &lt;code&gt;use&lt;/code&gt; hook), Chapter 101 (Fiber), or Chapter 108 (hydration mismatches) if those are gaps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Developers moving beyond the web:&lt;/strong&gt; Start at Chapter 121.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Link
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://tuts.kandz.me/category/programming/frontend-fed/react-ecosystem/react/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React — KandZ Tuts&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Free, no signup, 131 chapters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've been writing React for years but never understood what the scheduler does, or you're starting from scratch and want a path that doesn't stop at &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, this is the most complete free curriculum I've found.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;What React topics do you wish had better free resources? Let me know in the comments.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most AI support agents answer in paragraphs. Your user asks "how do I export my contacts?" and gets three sentences telling them to open Settings, then Data, then Export. Then they go hunting for each button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build &lt;a href="https://clickalong.ai/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=listing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clickalong&lt;/a&gt;, an AI support agent that answers from your docs and then points at each button in your app until the task is done. This post shows how to add it to a SaaS app, from a plain HTML page up to a React app with signed-in users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Create a workspace and load your docs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sign up at clickalong.ai and paste your help center link. Clickalong imports the articles, and the agent answers only from them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No help center yet? Write articles in the dashboard, or let your coding agent draft them with our open-source skill:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx skills add clickalong/clickalong-agent-skills --skill write-clickalong-docs
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


2. Add the widget
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On a plain HTML site, one tag goes before &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;/body&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://clickalong.ai/widget.js"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data-key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"pk_your_key"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;data-clickalong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Your key is on the Install page in the dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. In a React app, use the SDK
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bundled apps get a typed loader, &lt;code&gt;@clickalong/sdk&lt;/code&gt;. It has no dependencies, does nothing during server rendering, and is safe under React Strict Mode.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install @clickalong/sdk
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"use client"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clickalong&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@clickalong/sdk"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ClickalongWidget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clickalong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"pk_your_key"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clickalong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;identify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;email&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Render it once in your root layout: &lt;code&gt;app/layout.tsx&lt;/code&gt; in Next.js, &lt;code&gt;app/root.tsx&lt;/code&gt; in React Router. Conversations now follow the user across devices, and your team sees who they're talking to.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Want the Verified badge? Pass a &lt;code&gt;userHash&lt;/code&gt; as well. It's an HMAC-SHA256 of the lowercased email, computed on your server with the identity secret from Settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Pass app state
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Clickalong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"pro"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seats&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These values show next to the conversation, so whoever picks up a handed-over chat sees the user's plan without asking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Teach it the clicks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part that makes it more than a chatbot. Install the &lt;a href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/clickalong-recorder/mlmmampfbhgplpnblnhfpidmficcifcb" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clickalong Recorder&lt;/a&gt; Chrome extension, click through a task once (say, changing a plan), and save it as a tour. Next time a user asks how to change their plan, the agent answers and then walks them through it, one highlighted button at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No recorded tour for a task? The agent can still guide from your help articles with "Show me".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What you get
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An AI support agent that answers from your docs, in the user's language&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Guided tours that point at the real buttons in your app&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A shared inbox for chats the AI hands over&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One flat price per workspace with unlimited conversations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The widget and inbox are free. The AI agent starts on the Mini plan, and guided tours and "Show me" start on Essentials. &lt;a href="https://clickalong.ai/pricing?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=listing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;See pricing&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://clickalong.ai/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=listing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;start free&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgnpuvjnw0s9tzcddim9v.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgnpuvjnw0s9tzcddim9v.png" alt="A card titled Front door, marked In the replies, with three checked rows: Approved, Invited, Added to the group. Headline: One by one, all day." width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally posted October 30, 2026 on &lt;a href="https://elijahpaul.io/writing/fixing-the-front-door/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;elijahpaul.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TLDR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Telegram onboarding has been the biggest pain since launch. We're swapping it out. Meanwhile, the people already inside are building on top of Hot Bot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Front Door Is Broken
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lots of people have been approved for days and still see "not approved" when they check through Telegram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We tested it. The likely culprit: connecting Telegram from inside the X app instead of an external browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workaround for now:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open the link in an external browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If that still fails, verify with your wallet instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not good enough. So we're replacing the onboarding flow entirely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My words, mid-morning: "it was a bigger pain than we thought it would be."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Approving by Hand
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Until the new flow lands, we're in the replies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Approved. Invited. Added to the group. One by one, all day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Team no sleep, but making sure everyone is looked after.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Room Inside
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/elijahintek/status/2116183752854609928" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Favorite thing&lt;/a&gt; about the Hot Bot community: everyone's working together to make it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Strategies. MCP setups. Ideas for new features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Absolute euphoric feeling vs being stranded in the Cold War of the PvP bundlers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Built On Top
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of our users, &lt;a href="https://x.com/0xJohnB/status/2116283337799028985" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;@0xJohnB&lt;/a&gt;, built an anti-rug dashboard that reads his Hot Bot team through our MCP API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Strategies, trades and activity in one view, next to his own anti-rug checks. Read-only by design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running on a Raspberry Pi 5 at home.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the point of exposing the stack. Cooking with gas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Paper First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We push most users to trade on paper first. On purpose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Get a feel for Hot Bot without losing money. Then give us feedback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Subscriptions cover our costs. Proper software business.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Red Day
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whole market was down today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avg day in crypto. We ride.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Long on vision. Relentless on daily execution. 🔥🤖&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Elijah Paul, @elijahintek&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Co-founder of Hot Bot. Scottsdale, AZ&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;God calls us to serve others. I try to live that in business and in life.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First posted on &lt;a href="https://elijahpaul.io/writing/fixing-the-front-door/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;elijahpaul.io&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The log said the sync was scheduled. Two minutes later, nothing had run. No error, no rejected promise, no missed callback warning — just a line that promised future work and a database that never changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the excerpt that ended a week of blaming my own debounce logic:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[10:41:02] change detected
[10:41:02] sync scheduled in 120000ms
[10:41:32] service worker terminated (idle)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The timer did not fail. The process that owned it was shut down, and a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; cannot outlive its process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The short version&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; is a handle in the worker's heap. Terminate the worker and the handle is gone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;MV3 kills an idle background worker after about 30 seconds, no matter what is pending.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Persist the &lt;em&gt;due time&lt;/em&gt;, not the timer, and re-arm it when the worker wakes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Three suspects that were innocent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The debounce.&lt;/strong&gt; The code coalesced rapid changes into one sync two minutes later. It worked in tests because I kept clicking things, which kept the worker alive. In production, users typed once and walked away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A stale closure.&lt;/strong&gt; I rewrote the callback twice looking for a captured variable. The closure was fine. It simply never executed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Storage quota.&lt;/strong&gt; I added error handling around &lt;code&gt;chrome.storage&lt;/code&gt; and saw nothing, because nothing was written. The write happened inside the callback that never ran.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pattern behind all three: I was debugging JavaScript semantics when the failure was in the execution model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What MV3 actually guarantees
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome keeps a background service worker alive while it is handling events. When the event queue goes quiet for roughly 30 seconds, the worker is terminated. A single event may run for up to five minutes, but pending timers do not count as activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That means a timer longer than the idle window is a coin flip. It survives only if something else — a message, a click, an API call — keeps waking the worker before the reaper arrives. In a quiet extension, nothing does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fix 1: store the deadline, arm an alarm
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the default answer and it covers most features:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pending-sync-at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ALARM&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pending-sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;scheduleSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dueAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;storage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dueAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ALARM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dueAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onAlarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ALARM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;stored&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;storage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stored&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;storage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;local&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;remove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;runSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;runtime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onStartup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;recover&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two details matter. &lt;code&gt;alarms.create({ when })&lt;/code&gt; takes an absolute timestamp, so a worker restart does not shift the deadline. And the alarm handler clears the stored key before running the work, which prevents a duplicate run if the alarm fires twice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fix 2: run the delay where the user already is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the sync only matters while a tab is open, move the debounce into the content script. The page owns that timer, the worker is not involved, and closing the tab cancels work that no longer has a reason to exist. This is not a workaround; it is the right lifetime for UI-scoped work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fix 3: keep the worker warm (and accept the cost)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A message every 20 seconds keeps the idle timer from expiring. It also burns battery, keeps a process alive for no user benefit, and is the first thing a Chrome Web Store reviewer asks about. I have used it for a short migration window. I would not ship it as architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Fix 4: move the plumbing into the build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the codebase has dozens of timer call sites, rewriting each one by hand is how regressions multiply. &lt;code&gt;vite-mv3-sw-compile-persist&lt;/code&gt; rewrites long &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt; calls into alarm-backed wrappers at build time and restores marked state on wake. It requires Vite 8+, the &lt;code&gt;storage&lt;/code&gt; permission and &lt;code&gt;alarms&lt;/code&gt; when long timers are present; destructured variables and anonymous callbacks that capture locals are not recoverable, so the source still needs some discipline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more nuance: extension API calls reset the idle timer, so a worker that is making a request is not necessarily idle. Do not rely on that as a design, but it explains why a timer sometimes survives a burst of activity and dies in a quiet minute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


With and without the plugin
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The before/after from the plugin's README is the whole argument:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Without &lt;code&gt;vite-mv3-sw-compile-persist&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onClicked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;stored&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;storage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stored&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;storage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;delayInMinutes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onAlarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;alarm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;With &lt;code&gt;vite-mv3-sw-compile-persist&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;chrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onClicked&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The second version is the code you meant to write. The plugin adds restore, dirty tracking, a debounced flush and alarm-backed timers at build time — no runtime import, no wrapper API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/GameGC/vite-mv3-sw-compile-persist" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;vite-mv3-sw-compile-persist&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this saved you the afternoon I lost, a star on the repo is a fair trade — and it helps the next person find the explanation before the workaround.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What is the longest delay your extension promises, and what happens to that promise when the worker dies at second 30?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR for devs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start from the task, not from a popular reasoning pattern.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use a fixed workflow for predictable steps; consider model-generated plans when tasks vary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Other patterns: plan then execute, ReAct-style tool use, routing to specialists, parallel work, and draft-evaluate-revise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A plan is a proposal, not a guarantee, so validate actions and results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set stopping conditions (max steps, time limits) and let code handle exact arithmetic and rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fixed workflows, plan-then-execute, ReAct, routing, parallel work and draft-evaluate-revise&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask an AI assistant to “research three project management tools, compare their pricing, and recommend one for a five-person team.” A one-sentence answer may sound confident, but the task involves several pieces: identify suitable products, find current pricing, compare plans, consider the team’s needs, and present a recommendation with caveats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where planning patterns can help. They give an agent a structure for turning a goal into manageable steps. They can also make an application easier to debug: you can see what steps were attempted, what information was used, and where the process failed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Planning does not make an agent infallible. A model may misunderstand the goal, skip an important step, or rely on poor data. Developers need to choose a pattern that fits the task and add checks around tools and outputs. In this beginner-friendly guide, we’ll cover practical reasoning patterns, use a research example, and show a simple Python approach to a predictable plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Is Planning in an AI Agent?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Planning is the process of deciding what actions may be needed to reach a goal. An agent may decompose a request into subtasks, select tools, gather results, and decide whether it has enough information to finish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a research task, a plan might be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the team’s requirements from the user’s request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find three candidate products.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Visit or query reliable sources for current pricing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare plans that support five users.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Summarize trade-offs and state what could not be verified.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This plan is useful because it exposes the work behind the final recommendation. It also gives developers opportunities to validate inputs and constrain the agent. The system can require that pricing come from a source and include an “as of” date, for example.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://developers.openai.com/tracks/building-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenAI guide to building agents&lt;/a&gt; describes orchestration as handling multiple steps, tool use, handoffs, guardrails, and context. Anthropic’s &lt;a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Building Effective Agents&lt;/a&gt; distinguishes predefined workflows from more flexible agents. That distinction matters: not every task needs a model to invent its own sequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Start With the Task, Not a Reasoning Trend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake is to choose a popular pattern first and then try to fit a problem into it. Start by examining the task:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Are the steps predictable?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does the next step depend on information returned by the previous one?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Can independent parts run at the same time?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What mistakes would matter most?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does a human need to approve the outcome?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How will the system know it is finished?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the steps are fixed, ordinary code may be the best planner. For example, an invoice-processing workflow could validate the invoice, retrieve the supplier record, check required fields, and route it for review in a fixed order. A model could help extract information from the document, but the application can control the sequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a model-driven plan when the request varies enough that a fixed path is awkward, or when the agent must adapt based on retrieved information. Keep the model’s options bounded: define available tools, approval rules, and a clear stopping point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 1: A Fixed Workflow
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A fixed workflow follows steps selected by the developer. It is a strong fit for repeatable processes, such as generating a weekly report or checking an application form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A report workflow might be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Retrieve the latest sales data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Calculate totals and percentage changes in code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ask the model to explain the results in plain language.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Validate the required sections exist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Return a draft for review.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The benefit is predictability. If the task always follows the same route, the application can control the order and handle each failure explicitly. The trade-off is flexibility: a fixed workflow may be less convenient when users ask for a task that requires a different sequence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 2: Plan, Then Execute
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a plan-and-execute pattern, the model first proposes a series of steps. The application or another model call then carries them out, often with tools. This is useful when tasks vary but can still be decomposed into a list of actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the software comparison, the agent may propose gathering product candidates, retrieving pricing, comparing user limits, and composing a recommendation. The application can inspect the plan before execution. It could reject steps that use unapproved sources, ask for clarification about budget, or require confirmation before creating an account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plan is not inherently trustworthy. It may omit a necessary step or include one the application cannot perform. Validate each action against an allowlist and make tool results visible to the final synthesis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 3: ReAct-Style Tool Use
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A ReAct-style loop alternates between reasoning about the next step and taking an action, such as calling a search tool. The agent observes the result and decides what to do next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simplified loop is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Goal → choose next action → call tool → inspect result
→ choose another action or provide final answer
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This can be more adaptive than planning every step up front. If the first search finds that a product has changed its pricing page, the agent can adjust its next query. But the loop can also be less predictable. It may make unnecessary calls or keep searching after it has enough evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a production system, set limits on iterations, tool types, time, and cost. Log each tool request and result. Define conditions for asking the user a question or handing the task to a person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 4: Routing and Specialists
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A router classifies a request and sends it to a suitable workflow or specialist. A support system might route billing issues to a billing process and technical questions to a troubleshooting process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Routing can make instructions clearer and reduce the number of tools available to each path. It can also misclassify requests. Include an “uncertain” route, test borderline examples, and make it easy to hand a case to a human.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Specialists should have a distinct role. If two agents have the same tools and instructions, splitting them may only add complexity. Use specialists when the task genuinely needs different policies, tools, or expertise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 5: Parallel Work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some tasks can be divided into independent subtasks. For example, one process can collect pricing, another can compare collaboration features, and a third can check deployment options. The application can then combine the results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Parallel work can reduce waiting time or provide multiple perspectives, but it does not guarantee accuracy. The combined output needs a synthesis step that resolves disagreements and checks that sources support claims. Running several copies of a model on the same question can also multiply cost without adding useful diversity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use parallelization when the subtasks are genuinely independent and the results can be merged through a clear contract. Keep outputs structured enough for the application to compare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pattern 6: Draft, Evaluate, Revise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For tasks where quality can be checked against criteria, an agent can produce a draft, evaluate it, and revise it. A writing assistant might draft a report, check whether every claim has a source, and revise gaps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This pattern works best when the evaluator has concrete criteria. “Make it better” is vague. “Every pricing claim must include a current source; include at least two trade-offs; mark missing data as unknown” is easier to check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even a separate evaluator model can make mistakes. For factual claims, use deterministic checks or verify against source material where possible. Avoid endless critique loops by setting a maximum number of revisions and a fallback when the draft still fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Simple Python Example: A Predictable Research Plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a beginner project, you can represent the stages in ordinary Python rather than asking a model to generate an open-ended plan. Here, the research functions are placeholders; a real app would connect them to approved data sources.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataclasses&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataclass&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@dataclass&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;monthly_price_per_user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;source_url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;compare_tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;team_size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;dict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;team_size&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ValueError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;team_size must be at least 1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;comparisons&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;products&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;monthly_price_per_user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;team_size&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;monthly_price_per_user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;comparisons&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;append&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;team_monthly_cost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;source_url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source_url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;price_verified&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;source_url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;comparisons&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The code handles a repeatable calculation, while a model can help interpret the user’s criteria and explain trade-offs. Notice that missing prices remain &lt;code&gt;None&lt;/code&gt; instead of being guessed. This is a small example of a useful division of labor: models can help with language and flexible interpretation; ordinary code can perform exact calculations and enforce rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make Plans Observable and Testable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plan should help the developer understand what happened. Store or trace the steps at a level appropriate to your privacy policy: the selected tool, validated arguments, outcome, and reason for stopping. You do not need to expose private internal reasoning to users to make a system observable. A concise action log can explain that the agent checked three official pricing pages and found one plan without a public price.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Build test cases for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clear requests with enough information.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ambiguous goals that should trigger a question.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Missing or conflicting tool results.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A tool failure or timeout.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unsupported steps in a proposed plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Requests that should require approval.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Plans that exceed the allowed step limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Evaluate the result, not just the plan text. Did the agent use reliable sources? Did it calculate correctly? Did it stop when enough evidence was available? Did it distinguish verified facts from assumptions?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequent Planning Mistakes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Overplanning simple tasks
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a task takes one function call, a long agent loop adds latency and failure opportunities. Use a direct call or fixed function when it is enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Treating a plan as a guarantee
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A plan is a proposal. It can change, fail, or be based on incorrect assumptions. Validate actions and results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Giving the agent too many choices
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A huge tool list makes selection harder and expands the testing surface. Offer only tools relevant to the current task.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Forgetting stopping conditions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Specify maximum steps, time limits, and conditions that trigger a final answer, clarification, or human handoff.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Using a model for exact arithmetic
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let code handle totals, dates, eligibility, and other deterministic rules. The model can explain results but should not be the source of exact calculations when a program can compute them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does every AI agent need to make a plan?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. A simple agent may choose a tool and answer in one turn. Planning is useful when a task has multiple steps or dependencies, but it adds overhead and should be used when it improves the outcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is chain-of-thought the same as a plan?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. A plan is an actionable structure for completing a task. An application can show a concise plan or action summary without exposing private internal reasoning. Focus on observable actions and verifiable results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should I use a fixed workflow or a model-generated plan?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a fixed workflow when the steps are predictable. Consider a model-generated plan when the task varies and the model needs to choose among bounded options. Many systems combine both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I keep an agent from looping?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set maximum tool calls, timeouts, and explicit completion conditions. Detect repeated calls where possible and provide a clear fallback when the agent cannot finish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can multiple agents improve planning?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They can help when specialists have distinct roles or when independent perspectives are valuable. They also add coordination, latency, and cost. Start with one agent and split only when the workflow benefits from separation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Planning patterns help an AI agent organize multi-step work, but no single pattern is best for every task. Fixed workflows offer control, plan-and-execute supports flexible decomposition, tool-use loops adapt to new results, and parallel or evaluator patterns can help with independent subtasks and quality checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose the simplest approach that fits the task. Keep the agent’s tools and decisions bounded, use ordinary code for exact rules, set clear stopping conditions, and evaluate the full outcome. A useful plan is not the one that looks most sophisticated; it is the one that reliably helps the system reach a verifiable result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.openai.com/tracks/building-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenAI: Building agents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/agents/orchestration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenAI: Orchestration and handoffs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic: Building effective agents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I write about building AI agents and full-stack AI apps. Follow me for the next one, and tell me in the comments: Which planning pattern does your current agent use, and is it more complex than the task needs?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the past few weeks I've been building &lt;a href="https://hyroxpacer.site" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HYROX Pacer&lt;/a&gt; — a free, no-login web app that helps HYROX athletes plan their race: target finish time, 8×1km running splits, 8 station target times, and Roxzone transitions, all in one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HYROX is booming, but race planning is scattered across spreadsheets, forum posts and YouTube videos. I wanted one interactive tool where you pick your division (Open / Pro / Doubles), set a target time or pace, and get station-by-station splits you can actually race with.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What it does
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Target time &amp;amp; splits&lt;/strong&gt; — enter a goal finish time or pace and get per-lap and per-station targets&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Station calculators&lt;/strong&gt; — official HYROX weights built in (ski erg, sled push/pull, burpee broad jumps, farmers carry, wall balls and more)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Roxzone planner&lt;/strong&gt; — see how transition time changes your finish&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5K finish-time predictor&lt;/strong&gt; — turn a recent 5K into a realistic HYROX estimate&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Printable pace cards&lt;/strong&gt; — export wristband-style pace cards for race day&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;English &amp;amp; Chinese&lt;/strong&gt; interfaces&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Stack
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js (App Router) + React 19 + Tailwind CSS 4, deployed on Cloudflare Pages. No accounts and no paywall — everything runs client-side, so it stays fast and free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://hyroxpacer.site" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://hyroxpacer.site&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feedback welcome — especially from HYROX finishers: which splits do you actually want to see on race day?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After building over ten production projects with the Next.js App Router, I've accumulated a solid set of patterns that work and an equally long list of mistakes I won't repeat. This post is the article I wish I'd had when I first migrated from the Pages Router.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Server Components Are the Default — Embrace It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The single biggest mental shift is that components are server-rendered by default. This means you can fetch data directly inside any component without useEffect or client-side loading states. I now fetch data at the component level closest to where it's needed, keeping my pages lean. Only add 'use client' when you genuinely need interactivity — event handlers, hooks, browser APIs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Data Fetching Patterns That Work
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Use fetch() with Next.js cache options for data that changes infrequently&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Use generateStaticParams for dynamic routes with finite known values&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Prefer parallel data fetching with Promise.all to avoid waterfall requests&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Move auth checks into middleware for consistent protection across routes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;  Use React Suspense boundaries to progressively stream content to the client&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common Pitfalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most common mistake I see is importing server-only modules into client components. Next.js will throw a cryptic error that's hard to trace. Install the server-only package and import it at the top of any file that should never run in the browser. Another pitfall: don't put large client bundles in layout.tsx — that bundle will be sent on every page load. Keep layouts thin.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The App Router has a learning curve, but once it clicks you'll wonder how you lived without it. The combination of server components, streaming, and the new caching model makes Next.js apps faster and more maintainable than ever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you walk into the office of almost any growing wholesale distributor, textile supplier, or regional manufacturer today, you’ll see the exact same setup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sales rep sits in front of a desktop monitor with two windows open side by side: WhatsApp Web on the left, and a massive, color-coded Excel spreadsheet on the right.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the messages start coming in: a 40-second voice note or a blurry photo of a handwritten paper slip: &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Bro, send 40 cartons of the medium grade like last month, and let me know if charcoal is back in stock."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rep pauses the audio, flips over to Excel, hits &lt;code&gt;Ctrl+F&lt;/code&gt; to search the item, and sees stock listed as available. But the warehouse floor hasn't updated the sheet since Tuesday. So the rep calls the warehouse manager, waits on hold for ten minutes, confirms the numbers, and finally texts the buyer back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you handle five orders a day, this setup feels personal, flexible, and fine. But when you scale to forty orders a day, it turns into a complete profit killer.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


The Hidden Tax of "Free" Workflows
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most small-to-medium B2B business owners stick with WhatsApp and spreadsheets for one simple reason: they cost nothing out of pocket.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When software vendors pitch an upgrade, it’s almost always a rigid enterprise ERP or an e-commerce platform like Shopify Plus that demands $200 to $500 every month—plus extra per-seat user fees and transaction percentages. For a business operating on thin wholesale margins, paying thousands of dollars a year just so existing clients can submit orders makes zero financial sense.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So owners stay with manual entry. But "free" manual workflows carry steep operational costs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Transcription Error:&lt;/strong&gt; Every time someone copies a SKU, size code, or carton count from a chat thread into an invoice, typos happen. Shipping 500 meters of the wrong fabric roll or 50 units of the wrong industrial spare part wipes out the profit margin of your next ten shipments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Catalog Bottleneck:&lt;/strong&gt; Whenever prices or stock numbers change, you email or WhatsApp your buyers a 40MB PDF linesheet. Most never open it. They pull up the PDF they saved three months ago and place orders for items that have been discontinued.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The After-Hours Blindspot:&lt;/strong&gt; If a buyer wants to submit a restock list at 11:00 PM, they don’t want to text a rep and wait until 10:00 AM the next day to know if the warehouse can fulfill it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


The Lean Alternative: Custom B2B Portals
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over the past year building custom web systems for wholesale suppliers and manufacturers, I noticed a consistent pattern: businesses don’t actually want a massive, 50-feature ERP system that takes six months to onboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They only need three core functions to work reliably:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


1. A Live Digital Showroom (Not a Retail Checkout)
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;B2B buyers don't shop like retail consumers. They won't punch credit card details into a checkout form for a $4,000 order. They operate on credit terms, direct bank wire, or negotiated purchase orders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What they actually want is a clean web catalog where they can search by SKU, filter by material or variant in real time, see current inventory status, and assemble an order list in under two minutes on their phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


2. Strict Input Validation Before It Hits Your Desk
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a WhatsApp thread, a client can easily ask for "3 pieces" of an item that is only packed in cartons of 50.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a custom web portal with schema validation, the interface stops the error at the source. If the minimum order quantity is 50, the input only accepts increments of 50. By the time the order hits your sales desk, the line items, quantities, and specifications are 100% verified.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


3. Automated Routing to Your Existing Process
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don’t have to abandon WhatsApp if your team relies on it for client communication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most practical setups I deploy is a &lt;strong&gt;hybrid handoff&lt;/strong&gt;. The buyer selects their items and quantities in the web catalog and clicks &lt;em&gt;Submit Order Request&lt;/em&gt;. The application records the order in the database (updating stock levels immediately) and generates a clean, pre-formatted order summary routed straight to your sales desk's WhatsApp or internal dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can test how this zero-friction catalog and order handoff architecture works in practice through the live project demos on &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog-b2b-portal-vs-spreadsheets.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;my portfolio&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Why Custom Code Beats Monthly Subscriptions for SMEs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common question I hear from business owners is: &lt;em&gt;"Why shouldn't I just install a WordPress plugin or use a no-code builder?"&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It comes down to performance and long-term ownership:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Speed on Mobile Networks:&lt;/strong&gt; Template builders load dozens of unneeded scripts and third-party plugins. When a buyer tries to search 800 SKUs on a spotty mobile connection in an industrial district, bloated sites lag and fail. A custom frontend built on Next.js loads instantly because it only runs the code your workflow requires.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero Monthly License Fees:&lt;/strong&gt; You own the source code outright. Beyond basic cloud hosting—which is often free or a few dollars a month on modern tiers—you never pay recurring platform subscriptions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Built Around Your Floor Rules:&lt;/strong&gt; Whether you sell by yardage, weight, carton, or custom RFQ quote, custom software bends to your operational rules rather than forcing your warehouse staff to adapt to generic software.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


When to Upgrade
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your business processes &lt;strong&gt;fewer than five orders a day&lt;/strong&gt; with a small, static product list, stick to spreadsheets. Keep your overhead at zero.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, if your sales reps spend more than two hours every day transcribing chat messages, fielding "is this in stock?" calls, or resolving shipping mistakes, spreadsheets are no longer free. They are costing you revenue and growth.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I’m **Talha Javed&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;, a Full-Stack Developer specializing in custom Next.js and MERN web applications, B2B ordering portals, and internal operations dashboards for growing businesses.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Want to test live systems or discuss automating your current ordering workflow? Explore my recent work on *&lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog-b2b-portal-vs-spreadsheets.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;my portfolio&lt;/a&gt;** or reach out directly on &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.fiverr.com/s/L34y0W7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Fiverr&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; to scope out a tailored solution.*&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CRM stores '10:30' beside a follow-up task. One representative reads it in Jaipur and another in London. Neither knows which timezone the value represents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Time data needs to describe either an instant or a local scheduling intention. Those are different requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Store instants unambiguously
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An enquiry acceptance time is an instant. Store it using a timezone-aware database type or an explicit UTC representation supported by your system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript can serialize a valid Date as a UTC ISO string:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;acceptedAt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;acceptedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This records the browser or server clock's value. For authoritative acceptance time, prefer the backend's timestamp and maintain its clock synchronization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not infer an instant from an ambiguous string such as 06/07/2026 10:30.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Format for the reader
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Display an instant in an explicit timezone using a locale-aware formatter:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;display&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Intl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DateTimeFormat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en-GB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;dateStyle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;timeStyle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;short&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;timeZone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Asia/Kolkata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;acceptedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Choose the display timezone from a documented user or application setting. Label it where confusion is possible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Preserve scheduling intent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;'Call tomorrow at 9 AM in the customer's timezone' contains more than an instant. Store the selected local date and time together with an IANA timezone identifier, then resolve it for execution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recurring local schedules need their timezone rules preserved. A fixed UTC offset does not represent future daylight-saving changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some local times occur twice when clocks move backward; others do not occur when clocks move forward. Ask for or document a resolution policy rather than silently choosing an arbitrary instant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep audit fields separate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Meaning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Accepted at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backend accepted the enquiry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Event occurred at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Source reports when the event happened&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Received at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Integration received the event&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Next attempt at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Planned operational retry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A delayed event's receipt time is not its occurrence time. Avoid overwriting one with another.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test meaningful boundaries
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test different viewer timezones, month and year boundaries, daylight-saving transitions for relevant regions, invalid dates, and absent timezone settings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The conversion code above is illustrative and should be verified in your target runtime. Consistent timestamps make routing, follow-ups, and incident investigation easier because everyone can identify which moment a record describes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your page looks right on your laptop and scrolls sideways on your phone: this guide finds what is too wide and fixes the usual causes one at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The examples here are screenshots; they run live in &lt;a href="https://www.box-kite.dev/blog/a-layout-that-works-on-every-screen/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the original article&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You will build a small travel page that breaks on a phone the way real pages do, look at it the way a phone does, find the parts that stick out, and fix each one. At the end the same page is one column on a phone and two columns on a laptop. You need a React app with Box Kite installed; if you do not have one yet, steps 1 and 2 of &lt;a href="https://www.box-kite.dev/blog/start-building-with-box-kite/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Your first web page with Box Kite&lt;/a&gt; take two minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: build a page that breaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replace everything in &lt;code&gt;src/App.tsx&lt;/code&gt; with this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@box-kite/react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@box-kite/react/components/flex'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Aside&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;H1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Img&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;P&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ul&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@box-kite/react/components/semantics'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;photo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.box-kite.dev/img/blog/a-layout-that-works-on-every-screen/coast.jpg'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sections&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Itinerary'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Packing list'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Budget'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Photos'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Contacts'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Day 1 · Lisbon'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Day 4 · Sintra'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Day 7 · Lagos'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sections&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Link&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'#'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"indigo-600"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'indigo-300'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Link&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;gap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Aside&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;56&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;flexShrink&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Li&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Li&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ul&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Aside&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;H1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fontSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;32&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fontWeight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;700&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Ten&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Portugal&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;H1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Img&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;photo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;alt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'The coast near Lagos'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;P&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Tickets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;https&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//www.example.com/bookings/PT2026LISBONSINTRALAGOSK7Q2M9X4R8T1W6Z3N5V8B2C4&amp;lt;/P&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Flex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A row of links (indigo, and a lighter indigo in dark mode), a sidebar with a fixed width (&lt;code&gt;width={56}&lt;/code&gt; is 224 pixels, because spacing is on a scale of four), and a story with a photo and a booking link. On a wide laptop screen most of it looks fine. Every part of it is a real cause of a page that scrolls sideways.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.box-kite.dev%2Fimg%2Fblog%2Fa-layout-that-works-on-every-screen%2Fstarting-page.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.box-kite.dev%2Fimg%2Fblog%2Fa-layout-that-works-on-every-screen%2Fstarting-page.png" alt="The starting page: the sidebar keeps its width, and the photo runs past the edge of the frame."&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: look at it the way a phone does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You do not need a phone yet. Open your browser's developer tools (F12, or Cmd+Option+I on a Mac) and turn on the device toolbar: Ctrl+Shift+M, or Cmd+Shift+M on a Mac, in Chrome and Edge, and "Responsive Design Mode" in Firefox and Safari. Pick a phone such as the iPhone 12 Pro, which is 390 pixels wide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page now scrolls sideways: drag it left and the photo and the booking link keep going. That sideways scroll is the bug. A phone shows the page at its own width only because Vite's &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt; already carries the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;meta name="viewport"&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; line; keep it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: find what is too wide
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the Console tab of the developer tools, paste this and press Enter:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;querySelectorAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'body *'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getBoundingClientRect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;right&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;innerWidth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;scrollWidth&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;clientWidth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;outline&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'2px solid red'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every element that runs past the right edge of the screen, or whose content runs past its own edge, gets a red outline. The outer boxes light up because something inside them is too wide; look for the innermost red box and you have found the culprit. On this page there are three: the row of links, the sidebar with its photo beside it, and the booking link. Reload the page to clear the outlines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: let the row of links wrap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A row keeps everything on one line unless you tell it otherwise. Add &lt;code&gt;flexWrap="wrap"&lt;/code&gt; to the first &lt;code&gt;Flex&lt;/code&gt;, so the links that do not fit move to a second line:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Box&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@box-kite/react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
